      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501099,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Every property in object have a property descriptor.\nwhich we can inspect using this\nObject.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(person, \"name\"); and get\nthe output we see in image",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Every property in object have a property descriptor.\nwhich we can inspect using this Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(person, \"name\"); and get the output we see in image",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "WlA3hphJgyeJc_TjWjZyM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 18845.13338736168,
      "y": 655.6544151092612,
      "width": 2441.5414781571503,
      "height": 545.9999999999993,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5btAh",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 599490833,
      "version": 4958,
      "versionNonce": 566382253,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501099,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "High-Level Arrays Waste Space with \"Pointers\": Standard arrays and objects don't just hold your values—they store extra metadata, type\ntags, and memory addresses (pointers). The CPU has to jump to different places in memory just to collect the data, which wastes both\nRAM and processing cycles.\n\nWhy Heavy Tasks Crawl: For simple web forms, that pointer hopping doesn't matter. But for heavy workloads like streaming audio chunks\nfrom a TTS model, running games at 60 FPS, or processing image pixels chasing millions of scattered pointers creates lag, CPU cache\nmisses, and garbage collection freezes.\n\nThese Array buffer hold contiguous memory of only numbers (bits and bytes). You decide what those numbers mean—whether you run them\nthrough a text decoder for words, pass them to a sound card as audio waves, or send them to a GPU as image pixels.\n\none main point is if we have to read values byte by byte a normal array cannot do that we have to use unit8Array",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "High-Level Arrays Waste Space with \"Pointers\": Standard arrays and objects don't just hold your values—they store extra metadata, type tags, and memory addresses (pointers). The CPU has to jump to different places in memory just to collect the data, which wastes both RAM and processing cycles.\n\nWhy Heavy Tasks Crawl: For simple web forms, that pointer hopping doesn't matter. But for heavy workloads like streaming audio chunks from a TTS model, running games at 60 FPS, or processing image pixels chasing millions of scattered pointers creates lag, CPU cache misses, and garbage collection freezes.\n\nThese Array buffer hold contiguous memory of only numbers (bits and bytes). You decide what those numbers mean—whether you run them through a text decoder for words, pass them to a sound card as audio waves, or send them to a GPU as image pixels.\n\none main point is if we have to read values byte by byte a normal array cannot do that we have to use unit8Array",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "vm00N6DBA-vMG1CwKsEpt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 17161.800054028346,
      "y": 3908.9877484425947,
      "width": 1371.5414781571503,
      "height": 454.99999999999943,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5btAj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2008585010,
      "version": 5548,
      "versionNonce": 1799221517,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501099,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ArrayBuffer says: \"Here is some raw memory.\"\n\nTypedArray says: \"I'll interpret this memory as these particular numeric\ntypes.\"\n\nNow just an example how raw 8 bytes of Arraybuffer works for different kind\nof typed array for :- \nunit8Array :- space for 8 elements\nunit16Array :- sapce for 4 elements\nunit32Array :- space for 2 elemetns",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ArrayBuffer says: \"Here is some raw memory.\"\n\nTypedArray says: \"I'll interpret this memory as these particular numeric types.\"\n\nNow just an example how raw 8 bytes of Arraybuffer works for different kind of typed array for :- \nunit8Array :- space for 8 elements\nunit16Array :- sapce for 4 elements\nunit32Array :- space for 2 elemetns",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Q8gIaDHeR8z3Hpvr_gIJ4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 21489.98032848668,
      "y": 3227.718863807854,
      "width": 1509.3222419739336,
      "height": 363.99999999999955,
      "angle": 0,
