    {
      "id": "W7nGivjvY4PnaHMh6RRtC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19351.02116258501,
      "y": 2433.4877484425947,
      "width": 1996.5414781571506,
      "height": 1319.4999999999984,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 269558766,
      "version": 7504,
      "versionNonce": 76976269,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "DataView is another view over an ArrayBuffer. But by this we can store data dynamically like we can say store\nsetUnit8(0 , 100) , setUnit16(1 , 500) now here we are saying in the same buffer add 100 starting with index 0\nand for second we are saying add 500 starting with index 1.\n\n- TypedArray :- \"Interpret this memory as a sequence of one particular numeric type.\"\n- DataView  \"Let me read/write different types at specific byte offsets, with control over byte order.\"\n\nHow memory work ? when we store 500 in a Arraybuffer(4) (store 4 bytes of data each bytes have 8 bit of\nspace). now one byte can store 0 to 255 numbers (256 numbers).\nso , we do 500 / 256 = 1 quentiont and we get 244 as reminder.\nso for 1st bit we get 244 (in binary) and for second bit we get 1 (in binary). and when we print the buffer we\nget values in hex which is F4 01. F4 means 244 and 01 means 1 \nwhen we do \" const view16 = new Uint16Array(buffer); console.log(view16[0]);\" we get 500. now the default\nEndianness layout is little-endian.\n\nEndianness\n\nSuppose we have two bytes: 01 F4 How do we know whether that means: 500 or something else? Because\ncomputers need to know which byte comes first when storing multi-byte numbers. There are two common ways:-\n\n- Big-endian :- Most significant byte first: 01 F4\n- Little-endian:- Least significant byte first: F4 01\n\nnow when we are setting data at a buffer. we also have a option to choose in which layout we have to set the\ndata like view.setUint16(0, 500, true); The third argument: true means: Use little-endian. and for false use Big-\nendian and when we are reading the data we do view.getUint16(0, true) true means little-endian vice verca.\n\nWhy does this matter in real life? Because binary formats and network protocols have specific layouts. If you\ninterpret it using the wrong byte order, you'll get a completely different number.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "DataView is another view over an ArrayBuffer. But by this we can store data dynamically like we can say store setUnit8(0 , 100) , setUnit16(1 , 500) now here we are saying in the same buffer add 100 starting with index 0 and for second we are saying add 500 starting with index 1.\n\n- TypedArray :- \"Interpret this memory as a sequence of one particular numeric type.\"\n- DataView  \"Let me read/write different types at specific byte offsets, with control over byte order.\"\n\nHow memory work ? when we store 500 in a Arraybuffer(4) (store 4 bytes of data each bytes have 8 bit of space). now one byte can store 0 to 255 numbers (256 numbers).\nso , we do 500 / 256 = 1 quentiont and we get 244 as reminder.\nso for 1st bit we get 244 (in binary) and for second bit we get 1 (in binary). and when we print the buffer we get values in hex which is F4 01. F4 means 244 and 01 means 1 \nwhen we do \" const view16 = new Uint16Array(buffer); console.log(view16[0]);\" we get 500. now the default Endianness layout is little-endian.\n\nEndianness\n\nSuppose we have two bytes: 01 F4 How do we know whether that means: 500 or something else? Because computers need to know which byte comes first when storing multi-byte numbers. There are two common ways:-\n\n- Big-endian :- Most significant byte first: 01 F4\n- Little-endian:- Least significant byte first: F4 01\n\nnow when we are setting data at a buffer. we also have a option to choose in which layout we have to set the data like view.setUint16(0, 500, true); The third argument: true means: Use little-endian. and for false use Big-endian and when we are reading the data we do view.getUint16(0, true) true means little-endian vice verca.\n\nWhy does this matter in real life? Because binary formats and network protocols have specific layouts. If you interpret it using the wrong byte order, you'll get a completely different number.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "sUNbdnJ573zNZ2Nt8tyc8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 19340.521871014294,
      "y": 1603.6449665686534,
      "width": 1971.5414781571506,
      "height": 591.4999999999993,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1088342653,
      "version": 5983,
      "versionNonce": 650667043,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        },
        {
          "id": "WZ3mOj2a_Buj8l26y0HZo",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501139,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A Blob is used to package data into one immutable object, like sealing the data inside a box. Once the Blob is\ncreated, we cannot directly modify its contents. We can also give the Blob a MIME type, such as image/png,\naudio/wav, or text/plain, so APIs know what kind of data it represents. \n\nThe main difference between a TypedArray and a Blob is that a TypedArray is mainly used to access, read, and\nmodify individual bytes in an ArrayBuffer, while a Blob is used when the data is ready to be treated as one\nimmutable piece of data that can be uploaded, downloaded, displayed, or passed to another API.\n\nNow there is one more thing new File([imageBlob], \"photo.png\") which is a extend of Blob class So all the things\nyou can do with a Blob, you can generally do with a File. \nwe use this when we use: <input type=\"file\" accept=\"image/*\"/> When the user selects any file the browser gives\nyou a File object. That's why your frontend code can do things like: img.file and then pass that file to:\nFormData for your Express/Multer upload.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A Blob is used to package data into one immutable object, like sealing the data inside a box. Once the Blob is created, we cannot directly modify its contents. We can also give the Blob a MIME type, such as image/png, audio/wav, or text/plain, so APIs know what kind of data it represents. \n\nThe main difference between a TypedArray and a Blob is that a TypedArray is mainly used to access, read, and modify individual bytes in an ArrayBuffer, while a Blob is used when the data is ready to be treated as one immutable piece of data that can be uploaded, downloaded, displayed, or passed to another API.\n\nNow there is one more thing new File([imageBlob], \"photo.png\") which is a extend of Blob class So all the things you can do with a Blob, you can generally do with a File. \nwe use this when we use: <input type=\"file\" accept=\"image/*\"/> When the user selects any file the browser gives you a File object. That's why your frontend code can do things like: img.file and then pass that file to: FormData for your Express/Multer upload.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "r0dDjVT16AarcbpHK8JVV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 21467.064161254613,
      "y": 1553.6654025231028,
      "width": 1551.5414781571506,
      "height": 181.99999999999977,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0mV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 996012179,
      "version": 6155,
      "versionNonce": 1599279875,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        },
        {
          "id": "mXDgYCrEqjovGY52ja7JN",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501139,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Streaming is like connecting a pipe between a data source and a consumer. The source\nproduces chunks, those chunks travel through the pipe, and the consumer processes\nthem as they arrive instead of waiting for the entire data to arrive. You can stream\ndata over several things: -",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Streaming is like connecting a pipe between a data source and a consumer. The source produces chunks, those chunks travel through the pipe, and the consumer processes them as they arrive instead of waiting for the entire data to arrive. You can stream data over several things: -",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZrFQhRvwRvoJS3W7JVUOu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 21470.364242212603,
      "y": 1770.3320691897695,
      "width": 2008.2081448238177,
      "height": 682.4999999999991,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0ml",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 891723635,
      "version": 6971,
      "versionNonce": 883285933,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "95Z3emARVaFShZ4WHQPNS",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Two which we mainly uses :- \n - HTTP streaming: \"Here is a continuous flow of data in response to your request.\" \n - WebSocket: \"Let's keep a connection open so both sides can continuously send data to each other.\"\n\nA ReadableStream is when data is comming into chunks and we reading it one by one until it get finished \nA WritableStream is something you write data to.\n\nBackpressure\n\nIn a Stream Imagine : -\nThe producer is generating chunks: chunk 1 , chunk 2 , chunk 3 , ...  But the consumer is slower. The queue starts\nfilling. and this can blast your memory so we basically can apply backpressure, essentially telling the producer:\n\"The consumer isn't keeping up. Don't keep producing unlimited data.\"\n\nSo the flow becomes: Fast producer --> slow down --> manageable queue --> consumer processes",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Two which we mainly uses :- \n - HTTP streaming: \"Here is a continuous flow of data in response to your request.\" \n - WebSocket: \"Let's keep a connection open so both sides can continuously send data to each other.\"\n\nA ReadableStream is when data is comming into chunks and we reading it one by one until it get finished \nA WritableStream is something you write data to.\n\nBackpressure\n\nIn a Stream Imagine : -\nThe producer is generating chunks: chunk 1 , chunk 2 , chunk 3 , ...  But the consumer is slower. The queue starts filling. and this can blast your memory so we basically can apply backpressure, essentially telling the producer: \"The consumer isn't keeping up. Don't keep producing unlimited data.\"\n\nSo the flow becomes: Fast producer --> slow down --> manageable queue --> consumer processes",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
