        "type": 3
      },
      "seed": 423712516,
      "version": 177,
      "versionNonce": 2145353709,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "4PhzqvvFu8zhn7zASbCy5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 23904.622969822674,
      "y": -552.5821869070614,
      "width": 1478.3794836256802,
      "height": 227.49999999999972,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1195800124,
      "version": 5977,
      "versionNonce": 153287245,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "As we know Web Workers have separate memory have its own environment. The data\nis normally copied/cloned or, for transferable objects, ownership can be transferred.\nBut sometimes we don't want that.\nBoth threads need to work with the same piece of memory. That's what:\nSharedArrayBuffer is for.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "As we know Web Workers have separate memory have its own environment. The data is normally copied/cloned or, for transferable objects, ownership can be transferred. But sometimes we don't want that.\nBoth threads need to work with the same piece of memory. That's what: SharedArrayBuffer is for.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OdxLiu66XgsrkgU_-jd15",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26321.289636489342,
      "y": -520.0821869070614,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 955.4999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 34776692,
      "version": 6585,
      "versionNonce": 1369945283,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "0XdPzRWikaNiLROBOyR-b",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790445501139,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The problem: race condition as we have discussed previously \n\nAtomics solve this. It provides operations that are performed atomically. It basically means: This operation\nhappens as one indivisible operation. For example: Atomics.add(numbers, 0, 1); means: - Safely add 1 to numbers[0].\nAtomics only works with specific TypedArrays\n\nImportant Atomics methods\n\nAtomics.load() = reads value\nAtomics.store()  = safely stores value (Atomics.store(numbers, 0, 100))\n\nThere are also methods like : - Atomics.wait() , Atomics.notify()\n\nThese allow threads to wait for a change and wake other threads.\nThis is much better than constantly checking: - \nwhile (true) {\n    if (somethingChanged) {\n        // do work\n    }\n}\nbecause that kind of constant checking can waste CPU.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The problem: race condition as we have discussed previously \n\nAtomics solve this. It provides operations that are performed atomically. It basically means: This operation happens as one indivisible operation. For example: Atomics.add(numbers, 0, 1); means: - Safely add 1 to numbers[0].\nAtomics only works with specific TypedArrays\n\nImportant Atomics methods\n\nAtomics.load() = reads value\nAtomics.store()  = safely stores value (Atomics.store(numbers, 0, 100))\n\nThere are also methods like : - Atomics.wait() , Atomics.notify()\n\nThese allow threads to wait for a change and wake other threads.\nThis is much better than constantly checking: - \nwhile (true) {\n    if (somethingChanged) {\n        // do work\n    }\n}\nbecause that kind of constant checking can waste CPU.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "B1JrWxwKshlAkn-WrzvCq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26316.289636489342,
      "y": -960.0821869070614,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 181.99999999999977,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 919770842,
      "version": 6768,
      "versionNonce": 1148750605,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "OffscreenCanvas allows us to transfer the control of a normal <canvas> from the main thread to a Web Worker\nusing transferControlToOffscreen(). The worker can then perform the canvas rendering, while the result is still\ndisplayed through the original canvas on the main thread. This helps move heavy canvas rendering work away from\nthe main thread and keeps the UI more responsive.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "OffscreenCanvas allows us to transfer the control of a normal <canvas> from the main thread to a Web Worker using transferControlToOffscreen(). The worker can then perform the canvas rendering, while the result is still displayed through the original canvas on the main thread. This helps move heavy canvas rendering work away from the main thread and keeps the UI more responsive.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "VkOP43JFXUuHT-LcUdV65",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26316.289636489342,
      "y": -1418.231235410271,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 227.49999999999972,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17x",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 19207174,
      "version": 7104,
      "versionNonce": 1756209517,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "WebSocket is a communication protocol that provides a persistent, two-way connection between client and server.\n\nSocket.IO is a library that provides a higher-level real-time communication system; it can use WebSocket as a\ntransport when available, but it also has its own protocol and features such as events, rooms, broadcasting, and\nreconnection.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "WebSocket is a communication protocol that provides a persistent, two-way connection between client and server.\n\nSocket.IO is a library that provides a higher-level real-time communication system; it can use WebSocket as a transport when available, but it also has its own protocol and features such as events, rooms, broadcasting, and reconnection.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "YDXqOhibrzGIC7F2HN-lv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24317.289636489342,
      "y": 2377.1973360183,
      "width": 1816.7128169590144,
      "height": 955.4999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 407664462,
      "version": 7693,
      "versionNonce": 1634135469,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790450644364,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "First understand Which data goes where and how it get stored :- \n\nThe engine steals the lowest bit as a \"tag\".\n\n - If the tag bit is 1, it is a pointer to the heap.\n - If the tag bit is 0, the remaining bits are the actual integer value directly in the slot.\n\nNow Small Integers (SMI) get directly inside the variable's slot and large number also usually uses\nHeaps\n\nQ . Where do true, false, null, and undefined fit?\nIn V8, true, false, null, and undefined are actually treated as pointers (1 tag) that point to permanent\nsingletons in the heap (often called Oddballs).\n\n - There is exactly one true object living in the engine's memory.\n - If 1,000 variables are set to true, all 1,000 variables just hold the pointer ending in 1 that points\nto that single true object in the heap.\n\nQ . How let x = \"shivam\" Works\n\nA string is stored as a single, continuous block of bytes on the heap",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "First understand Which data goes where and how it get stored :- \n\nThe engine steals the lowest bit as a \"tag\".\n\n - If the tag bit is 1, it is a pointer to the heap.\n - If the tag bit is 0, the remaining bits are the actual integer value directly in the slot.\n\nNow Small Integers (SMI) get directly inside the variable's slot and large number also usually uses Heaps\n\nQ . Where do true, false, null, and undefined fit?\nIn V8, true, false, null, and undefined are actually treated as pointers (1 tag) that point to permanent singletons in the heap (often called Oddballs).\n\n - There is exactly one true object living in the engine's memory.\n - If 1,000 variables are set to true, all 1,000 variables just hold the pointer ending in 1 that points to that single true object in the heap.\n\nQ . How let x = \"shivam\" Works\n\nA string is stored as a single, continuous block of bytes on the heap",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "40NZQ42kfLwF7vvdnI5TU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 25184.956303156006,
      "y": 3343.1973360183,
      "width": 2110.712816959015,
      "height": 500.4999999999994,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 322244803,
      "version": 7861,
      "versionNonce": 197809645,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790450734776,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What happens if you do x[0]?\nWhen you write let firstLetter = x[0];:\n\nThe engine follows the pointer in x to the string in the heap.\n\nIt looks at index 0 and reads the byte for 's'.\n\nIt creates or retrieves a 1-character string for 's' (engines even keep an internal cache of all 256 single-character\nASCII strings to make this instantaneous).\n\nfirstLetter now gets a pointer to that 's'.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What happens if you do x[0]?\nWhen you write let firstLetter = x[0];:\n\nThe engine follows the pointer in x to the string in the heap.\n\nIt looks at index 0 and reads the byte for 's'.\n\nIt creates or retrieves a 1-character string for 's' (engines even keep an internal cache of all 256 single-character ASCII strings to make this instantaneous).\n\nfirstLetter now gets a pointer to that 's'.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "IO9Vssm-p7g-WhplAr1Dz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24331.62296982267,
      "y": 3914.864002684967,
      "width": 2953.2128169590133,
      "height": 272.99999999999966,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2020517635,
      "version": 8036,
      "versionNonce": 109254541,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790451936903,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q . Now What is a Heap ?\n\nThe Heap is a giant warehouse floor behind the office.\nUnlike the stack, which must follow strict \"first in, last out\" order, the heap has no order. If you need space for a huge object or a long sentence, the engine walks\ninto the warehouse, finds an empty corner big enough, puts your data down, and gives you a note: \"Your stuff is at Bay #4102.\" That slip of paper with the address\nis your reference / pointer.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q . Now What is a Heap ?\n\nThe Heap is a giant warehouse floor behind the office.\nUnlike the stack, which must follow strict \"first in, last out\" order, the heap has no order. If you need space for a huge object or a long sentence, the engine walks into the warehouse, finds an empty corner big enough, puts your data down, and gives you a note: \"Your stuff is at Bay #4102.\" That slip of paper with the address is your reference / pointer.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7qLklM0y3vJvECI8VcKoa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26161.289636489342,
      "y": 2314.864002684967,
      "width": 1120.7128169590144,
      "height": 45.49999999999994,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 39270957,
      "version": 7787,
      "versionNonce": 34193421,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790450267539,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Which data do where",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Which data do where",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "SEKjeQqjEBRk2uvL3SPGE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24348.789636489342,
      "y": 4444.864002684967,
      "width": 2036.712816959016,
      "height": 773.4999999999991,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 275455900,
      "version": 8139,
      "versionNonce": 2046828077,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790452523775,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Garbage Collection (GC) automatically manages memory by finding which objects in the heap are still reachable from\nGC roots. \n\nGC roots are starting points such as global references and references held by currently executing code. The\ngarbage collector follows the references from these roots, forming a graph of reachable objects. Any heap object\nthat can be reached through this chain is considered alive and is kept.\nObjects that cannot be reached from any GC root are considered unreachable, even if they reference other objects\nlike in the example see in the user we have another object which is address when the user get null the connection\nof address get breaks from the GC roots and their memory can eventually be reclaimed by the garbage collector.\nIn a mark-and-sweep (Its a algorithm) style process, the GC first marks reachable objects ( Marks means which\nvalues are gonna stay and which are gonna sweep out ) and then sweeps/reclaims memory belonging to unreachable\nobjects.\n\nThe real GC in V8 isn't simply: MARK everything --> STOP --> SWEEP everything\n\nV8 uses more sophisticated techniques, including generational garbage collection, and performs different kinds of\ncollection depending on the situation.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Garbage Collection (GC) automatically manages memory by finding which objects in the heap are still reachable from GC roots. \n\nGC roots are starting points such as global references and references held by currently executing code. The garbage collector follows the references from these roots, forming a graph of reachable objects. Any heap object that can be reached through this chain is considered alive and is kept.\nObjects that cannot be reached from any GC root are considered unreachable, even if they reference other objects like in the example see in the user we have another object which is address when the user get null the connection of address get breaks from the GC roots and their memory can eventually be reclaimed by the garbage collector.\nIn a mark-and-sweep (Its a algorithm) style process, the GC first marks reachable objects ( Marks means which values are gonna stay and which are gonna sweep out ) and then sweeps/reclaims memory belonging to unreachable objects.\n\nThe real GC in V8 isn't simply: MARK everything --> STOP --> SWEEP everything\n\nV8 uses more sophisticated techniques, including generational garbage collection, and performs different kinds of collection depending on the situation.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3kydE8aUhHiz4mJ-rJxuM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 27452.122969822674,
      "y": 4989.864002684967,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 318.4999999999996,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1980662564,
      "version": 7732,
      "versionNonce": 1590645283,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790452020644,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Memory that is no longer logically needed by the application but remains reachable, preventing the garbage collector\nfrom reclaiming it.\n\nA memory leak happens when:\n\n - Application: \"I don't need this anymore.\"\n - GC: \"But it's still reachable, so I can't remove it.\"",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Memory that is no longer logically needed by the application but remains reachable, preventing the garbage collector from reclaiming it.\n\nA memory leak happens when:\n\n - Application: \"I don't need this anymore.\"\n - GC: \"But it's still reachable, so I can't remove it.\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3gkG-yksW5CwdU483DShB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 27432.95630315601,
      "y": 3856.146027946281,
      "width": 1550.0461502923451,
      "height": 272.99999999999966,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1136438044,
      "version": 7992,
      "versionNonce": 1537544547,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790452015856,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Basically, Map is used to store key-value pairs, and its key can be either a primitive\nvalue like a string, number, or boolean, or a non-primitive value like an object, array, or\nfunction. Suppose we store an object as a key in a Map. Even if we later make the\nvariable user null, the object is still not eligible for garbage collection because the Map\nitself is holding a strong reference to that object. We have only removed the reference\nfrom the user variable; the object is still reachable through the Map",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Basically, Map is used to store key-value pairs, and its key can be either a primitive value like a string, number, or boolean, or a non-primitive value like an object, array, or function. Suppose we store an object as a key in a Map. Even if we later make the variable user null, the object is still not eligible for garbage collection because the Map itself is holding a strong reference to that object. We have only removed the reference from the user variable; the object is still reachable through the Map",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8iruoF0Sg_MoMJK2frjLI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24365.622969822674,
      "y": 6359.646027946281,
      "width": 1708.5461502923467,
      "height": 272.99999999999966,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xyO",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 584343460,
      "version": 8246,
      "versionNonce": 900137773,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790453384141,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "V8 is a JavaScript engine developed by Google. It is used by: Chrome Node.js Deno and other\nenvironments\n\nV8 is the JavaScript engine that parses, executes, optimizes JavaScript and manages runtime\nthings such as memory and garbage collection. V8 is not JavaScript itself. JavaScript = language\n, V8 = engine that executes JavaScript",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "V8 is a JavaScript engine developed by Google. It is used by: Chrome Node.js Deno and other environments\n\nV8 is the JavaScript engine that parses, executes, optimizes JavaScript and manages runtime things such as memory and garbage collection. V8 is not JavaScript itself. JavaScript = language , V8 = engine that executes JavaScript",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "CkzB7mDZSvTZnK1o_0YeW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24396.83142117645,
      "y": 7256.141071087794,
      "width": 1658.5461502923467,
      "height": 227.49999999999972,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xyt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1792924828,
      "version": 8525,
      "versionNonce": 2040010125,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790453384141,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "So you can think : - Parser = understands the structure/grammar of the JavaScript code.\n\nThe first thing V8 needs to do is understand the structure of your JavaScript code. That's\nthe job of the parser. A parser looks at code and understands things like:\nIt checks whether the code follows JavaScript's syntax.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "So you can think : - Parser = understands the structure/grammar of the JavaScript code.\n\nThe first thing V8 needs to do is understand the structure of your JavaScript code. That's the job of the parser. A parser looks at code and understands things like:\nIt checks whether the code follows JavaScript's syntax.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "0hj32sZoVKGD2Hvk24G2a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24402.83142117645,
      "y": 8052.141071087794,
      "width": 1658.5461502923467,
      "height": 318.4999999999996,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xyx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1830651036,
      "version": 8621,
      "versionNonce": 376953837,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790453384141,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "While parsing the code, V8 creates something called an AST ( Abstract Syntax Tree ) It\nbasically represents your code as a structured tree.\n\nWhy do we need this? Because working with a structured representation is much easier for\nthe engine than directly working with raw text.\n\nIt's an internal representation that helps the engine understand your code.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "While parsing the code, V8 creates something called an AST ( Abstract Syntax Tree ) It basically represents your code as a structured tree.\n\nWhy do we need this? Because working with a structured representation is much easier for the engine than directly working with raw text.\n\nIt's an internal representation that helps the engine understand your code.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "4Boj6jX3-oB5AQSFQ99Tn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26545.7510511548,
      "y": 8383.641390063927,
      "width": 1960.4570379971115,
      "height": 90.99999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xyy",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2066017165,
      "version": 8830,
      "versionNonce": 1682953805,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "nN2ycKZ-Y8dZJCxcLd82R",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790453384141,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Bytecode is a lower-level set of instructions created from our AST. It is easier for the JavaScript engine to\nwork with than the original JavaScript, but it is not machine code, so the CPU cannot directly execute it.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Bytecode is a lower-level set of instructions created from our AST. It is easier for the JavaScript engine to work with than the original JavaScript, but it is not machine code, so the CPU cannot directly execute it.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "dPxveb4gqicNS8ieJVwoG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26553.945419831874,
      "y": 7969.702405933892,
      "width": 1967.1563755778084,
      "height": 136.49999999999983,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xyyV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 96394403,
      "version": 8901,
      "versionNonce": 1730045709,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "wy8gurO7UbscraV1x_jGd",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790453384141,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An interpreter is a program that executes the bytecode. In V8, the interpreter is called Ignition. It reads the\nbytecode instructions and performs the operations they represent using machine-level instructions that the\nCPU can execute.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An interpreter is a program that executes the bytecode. In V8, the interpreter is called Ignition. It reads the bytecode instructions and performs the operations they represent using machine-level instructions that the CPU can execute.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "vU77O3MB9KtdYxnDzDGss",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26552.435075142526,
