      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "-4b-hTjjlhcjEwg5iRqs7",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790453384141,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Basically, bytecode is a lower-level representation of our JavaScript code, but it is not machine code that the CPU\ncan directly understand. The interpreter is a program that is already compiled into machine code, so the CPU can\nexecute the interpreter itself. The interpreter reads the bytecode instructions one by one and knows what each\ninstruction means and what operation needs to be performed. So the flow is bytecode → interpreter → CPU: the\ninterpreter understands and executes the bytecode, while the CPU actually executes the machine-level instructions\ninside the interpreter.\n\nTraditionally, JavaScript was described as an interpreted language because JavaScript engines primarily used an\ninterpreter to execute the code. But modern JavaScript engines like V8 also use JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation.\nWhen some code is executed repeatedly, it becomes hot code, and V8 can compile that code into optimized machine\ncode. But why is compiled code faster? Basically, compiled code has already been translated into machine code that\nthe CPU can execute directly, so the interpreter does not have to repeatedly read and execute each bytecode\ninstruction. This removes the extra interpretation overhead and makes the execution faster.\n\nSo, you might wonder: why don't we just compile the whole JavaScript code when the webpage loads? The problem is\nthat compilation itself takes time and uses memory, and a webpage may contain a lot of JavaScript that might run\nonly once or might never run at all. If we compiled everything immediately, the webpage could take longer to start.\n\nThat's why modern engines like V8 use a balance: they can start executing code relatively quickly using bytecode +\ninterpretation, and when they detect code that is executed frequently, they can use JIT compilation to turn that\ncode into optimized machine code. This gives us a balance between fast startup and good execution performance.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Basically, bytecode is a lower-level representation of our JavaScript code, but it is not machine code that the CPU can directly understand. The interpreter is a program that is already compiled into machine code, so the CPU can execute the interpreter itself. The interpreter reads the bytecode instructions one by one and knows what each instruction means and what operation needs to be performed. So the flow is bytecode → interpreter → CPU: the interpreter understands and executes the bytecode, while the CPU actually executes the machine-level instructions inside the interpreter.\n\nTraditionally, JavaScript was described as an interpreted language because JavaScript engines primarily used an interpreter to execute the code. But modern JavaScript engines like V8 also use JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation. When some code is executed repeatedly, it becomes hot code, and V8 can compile that code into optimized machine code. But why is compiled code faster? Basically, compiled code has already been translated into machine code that the CPU can execute directly, so the interpreter does not have to repeatedly read and execute each bytecode instruction. This removes the extra interpretation overhead and makes the execution faster.\n\nSo, you might wonder: why don't we just compile the whole JavaScript code when the webpage loads? The problem is that compilation itself takes time and uses memory, and a webpage may contain a lot of JavaScript that might run only once or might never run at all. If we compiled everything immediately, the webpage could take longer to start.\n\nThat's why modern engines like V8 use a balance: they can start executing code relatively quickly using bytecode + interpretation, and when they detect code that is executed frequently, they can use JIT compilation to turn that code into optimized machine code. This gives us a balance between fast startup and good execution performance.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "S0C3zVXZ0TiIoxnGLFO5C",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 27433.94347723274,
      "y": 4158.942615442299,
      "width": 1550.0461502923451,
      "height": 363.99999999999955,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 226644508,
      "version": 8074,
      "versionNonce": 1549987075,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790452015856,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now WeakMap is similar to Map because it also stores key-value pairs, but its keys\nmust be objects (arrays and functions can also be keys because they are objects), not\nprimitives like strings, numbers, or booleans.\n\nThe important difference is that WeakMap holds the key weakly. So, if user = null and\nthere are no other strong references to that object, the object becomes unreachable\nand eligible for garbage collection, and eventually the object and its corresponding\nWeakMap entry can be removed.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now WeakMap is similar to Map because it also stores key-value pairs, but its keys must be objects (arrays and functions can also be keys because they are objects), not primitives like strings, numbers, or booleans.\n\nThe important difference is that WeakMap holds the key weakly. So, if user = null and there are no other strong references to that object, the object becomes unreachable and eligible for garbage collection, and eventually the object and its corresponding WeakMap entry can be removed.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "imbrwtqBClzFJFwAY6UOP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 27435.420955759535,
      "y": 4535.411038790032,
      "width": 2040.091135001525,
      "height": 90.99999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17xzV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 367007396,
      "version": 8227,
      "versionNonce": 740578467,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790452015856,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Because garbage collection can happen at an unpredictable time, WeakMap does not provide .size and does not allow\nus to iterate over its keys or entries like a normal Map.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Because garbage collection can happen at an unpredictable time, WeakMap does not provide .size and does not allow us to iterate over its keys or entries like a normal Map.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "BHZ3-CxdX6VzCEXL6tR4V",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 28657.622969822674,
      "y": -2988.2312354102714,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 682.4999999999991,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 146210694,
      "version": 7578,
      "versionNonce": 155074637,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A MediaStream represents a continuous flow of media data. It is not just one audio file, one video file, or a single\npiece of data; instead, it represents media that is continuously coming from a source such as a microphone or\ncamera.\n\nWhen we write const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true });, we are asking the\nbrowser to give us a MediaStream containing audio from the user's microphone. If we request both audio and\nvideo, for example getUserMedia({ audio: true, video: true }), the returned MediaStream can contain multiple\ntracks.\n\nEach track represents one individual media source: an AudioTrack represents audio from a microphone, while a\nVideoTrack represents video from a camera. We can access these tracks using stream.getAudioTracks() or\nstream.getVideoTracks().\n\nWe can then pass that stream or its tracks to other browser APIs such as the Web Audio API, WebRTC, or\nMediaRecorder depending on what we want to do with the media.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A MediaStream represents a continuous flow of media data. It is not just one audio file, one video file, or a single piece of data; instead, it represents media that is continuously coming from a source such as a microphone or camera.\n\nWhen we write const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true });, we are asking the browser to give us a MediaStream containing audio from the user's microphone. If we request both audio and video, for example getUserMedia({ audio: true, video: true }), the returned MediaStream can contain multiple tracks.\n\nEach track represents one individual media source: an AudioTrack represents audio from a microphone, while a VideoTrack represents video from a camera. We can access these tracks using stream.getAudioTracks() or stream.getVideoTracks().\n\nWe can then pass that stream or its tracks to other browser APIs such as the Web Audio API, WebRTC, or MediaRecorder depending on what we want to do with the media.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZKmHpMk2rItcx_JEOpsjt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 23927.622969822674,
      "y": -4931.5645687436045,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 864.4999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17yV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 605287366,
      "version": 7860,
      "versionNonce": 227192493,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When the browser renders a webpage, it first parses the HTML and creates the DOM (Document Object Model). At\nthe same time, when it discovers CSS, the browser downloads and parses it to create the CSSOM (CSS Object\nModel). The browser then combines the DOM and CSSOM to create the Render Tree, which contains the elements\nthat actually need to be rendered on the screen (for example, display: none elements are not included).\n\nNow creating this DOM and CSSOM is can be done in parallel\n\nNext comes Layout, where the browser calculates the exact position and size of each element, such as its width,\nheight, and where it should be placed on the page.\n\nAfter that comes Paint, where the browser actually draws the visual parts of those elements, such as text,\ncolors, backgrounds, borders, shadows, and images.\n\nThe browser can create separate layers for certain elements, so if a component moves from 0px to 100px, it can\nmove that layer instead of repainting the whole page just because one element moved. so composite is combines\nthe different painted layers together in the correct order to produce the final image/frame that appears on the\nscreen.\n\nDOM + CSSOM → Render Tree → Layout → Paint → Composite → Screen.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When the browser renders a webpage, it first parses the HTML and creates the DOM (Document Object Model). At the same time, when it discovers CSS, the browser downloads and parses it to create the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). The browser then combines the DOM and CSSOM to create the Render Tree, which contains the elements that actually need to be rendered on the screen (for example, display: none elements are not included).\n\nNow creating this DOM and CSSOM is can be done in parallel\n\nNext comes Layout, where the browser calculates the exact position and size of each element, such as its width, height, and where it should be placed on the page.\n\nAfter that comes Paint, where the browser actually draws the visual parts of those elements, such as text, colors, backgrounds, borders, shadows, and images.\n\nThe browser can create separate layers for certain elements, so if a component moves from 0px to 100px, it can move that layer instead of repainting the whole page just because one element moved. so composite is combines the different painted layers together in the correct order to produce the final image/frame that appears on the screen.\n\nDOM + CSSOM → Render Tree → Layout → Paint → Composite → Screen.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_m4bTfozs-AZ_TiNsMgRC",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26252.98145470131,
      "y": -2998.951832708154,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 136.49999999999983,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1634745050,
      "version": 7325,
      "versionNonce": 2130333965,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Web Audio API is a browser API that allows JavaScript to work with audio in a more advanced way. For\nexample, we can take audio from a file, microphone, or another source, process or analyze that audio, and finally\nsend it to the speakers or headphones.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The Web Audio API is a browser API that allows JavaScript to work with audio in a more advanced way. For example, we can take audio from a file, microphone, or another source, process or analyze that audio, and finally send it to the speakers or headphones.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kaZdV_UDflVPAAwSGL5My",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26259.871837588464,
      "y": -2842.799828404444,
      "width": 2030.0461502923447,
      "height": 90.99999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b17zV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 477720026,
      "version": 7375,
      "versionNonce": 990291821,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Everything starts by creating an AudioContext using new AudioContext(). You can think of the AudioContext as\nthe main audio engine that manages all the audio processing.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Everything starts by creating an AudioContext using new AudioContext(). You can think of the AudioContext as the main audio engine that manages all the audio processing.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "xobvAuSh-E_Qsrpv9vV7U",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 23906.218173279456,
      "y": -234.93319875936908,
      "width": 1478.3794836256802,
      "height": 181.99999999999977,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b18",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 638710276,
      "version": 6167,
      "versionNonce": 871969229,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "const buffer = new SharedArrayBuffer(8); This creates a shared memory area of:8\nbytes. Sending it to a worker. The worker receives a reference to the same shared\nmemory and You can then create a TypedArray over it at the both the side and\nmake chnages over it. ",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "const buffer = new SharedArrayBuffer(8); This creates a shared memory area of:8 bytes. Sending it to a worker. The worker receives a reference to the same shared memory and You can then create a TypedArray over it at the both the side and make chnages over it. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
