      "locked": false
    },
    {
      "id": "9aH6XDYBaiAycRz0fwpSA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26253.78388151502,
      "y": -2734.663296356431,
      "width": 1606.3360217030286,
      "height": 636.9999999999992,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1891169542,
      "version": 7748,
      "versionNonce": 1192068685,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501103,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "After creating it, we first decide where our audio will come from, which is called the source.\n\nFor example, an audio file can be a source, and a microphone can also be a source. Then we\ncan create different AudioNodes to process or analyze the audio. For example, a GainNode\nis used to control the volume, while an AnalyserNode is used to inspect the audio, such as\ngetting its frequency data for a music visualizer. We connect these nodes using .connect().\n\nThe connect() method basically creates a path for the audio to flow from one node to\nanother.\n\nFor example, source.connect(gainNode) means send the audio from the source into the\nGainNode. Then gainNode.connect(audioContext.destination) means send the output of\nthe GainNode to the final audio output, where audioContext.destination normally\nrepresents the speakers or headphones.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "After creating it, we first decide where our audio will come from, which is called the source.\n\nFor example, an audio file can be a source, and a microphone can also be a source. Then we can create different AudioNodes to process or analyze the audio. For example, a GainNode is used to control the volume, while an AnalyserNode is used to inspect the audio, such as getting its frequency data for a music visualizer. We connect these nodes using .connect().\n\nThe connect() method basically creates a path for the audio to flow from one node to another.\n\nFor example, source.connect(gainNode) means send the audio from the source into the GainNode. Then gainNode.connect(audioContext.destination) means send the output of the GainNode to the final audio output, where audioContext.destination normally represents the speakers or headphones.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "vEUAhPEL9cHVhZlphRvBx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 26252.78388151502,
      "y": -2084.663296356431,
      "width": 2139.6693550363643,
      "height": 409.4999999999995,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1239634714,
      "version": 7915,
      "versionNonce": 495006893,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790445501103,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We don't have to connect every node directly to the destination; instead, we create the path we actually want. For\nexample, if we want the audio to first go through the volume control and then through the analyser, we can do source →\ngainNode → analyser → destination\nIn code, that would be source.connect(gainNode), gainNode.connect(analyser), and\nanalyser.connect(audioContext.destination)\n\nWe can also create branches, where the same audio goes to multiple nodes. So overall, you can think of the Web Audio\nAPI as a pipeline or graph of audio nodes, where the audio starts from a source, flows through whatever processing or\nanalysis nodes we choose, and eventually reaches the destination such as the speakers.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We don't have to connect every node directly to the destination; instead, we create the path we actually want. For example, if we want the audio to first go through the volume control and then through the analyser, we can do source → gainNode → analyser → destination\nIn code, that would be source.connect(gainNode), gainNode.connect(analyser), and analyser.connect(audioContext.destination)\n\nWe can also create branches, where the same audio goes to multiple nodes. So overall, you can think of the Web Audio API as a pipeline or graph of audio nodes, where the audio starts from a source, flows through whatever processing or analysis nodes we choose, and eventually reaches the destination such as the speakers.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
